[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Advanced Django ORM: Custom Managers, QuerySets, and F/Q Expressions"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Django's Object-Relational Mapper (ORM) provides a powerful and abstracted way to interact with your database. However, as applications grow, keeping database logic inside your views or serializers leads to bloated code and duplication. This tutorial explores how to encapsulate database logic using Custom Managers, Chained QuerySets, and advanced atomic operations via F and Q expressions."},{"order":3,"type":"heading","text":"1. Custom Managers vs. Custom QuerySets"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"A Manager is the interface through which database query operations are provided to Django models. By default, every Django model has at least one Manager named objects. A Custom Manager allows you to add table-level methods, whereas a Custom QuerySet allows your methods to be chainable."},{"order":5,"type":"subheading","text":"Implementing a Custom QuerySet and Manager"},{"order":6,"type":"code","text":"from django.db import models

class ArticleQuerySet(models.QuerySet):
    def published(self):
        return self.filter(status='published')

    def by_author(self, author_name):
        return self.filter(author__name=author_name)

class ArticleManager(models.Manager):
    def get_queryset(self):
        return ArticleQuerySet(self.model, using=self._db)

    def published(self):
        return self.get_queryset().published()

    def by_author(self, author_name):
        return self.get_queryset().by_author(author_name)

class Article(models.Model):
    title = models.CharField(max_length=200)
    status = models.CharField(max_length=50, default='draft')
    author = models.ForeignKey('Author', on_delete=models.CASCADE)
    views = models.PositiveIntegerField(default=0)

    objects = ArticleManager()"},{"order":7,"type":"paragraph","text":"By mirroring QuerySet methods in the Manager, you ensure that calls like Article.objects.published().by_author('Jane Doe') work seamlessly while retaining full chainability."},{"order":8,"type":"heading","text":"2. Database-Level Operations with F Expressions"},{"order":9,"type":"paragraph","text":"An F expression represents the value of a model field or an annotated column. It allows you to make database operations happen in the database rather than in Python memory, preventing race conditions."},{"order":10,"type":"subheading","text":"Updating Fields Incrementally"},{"order":11,"type":"code","text":"from django.db.models import F

# Without F expression (prone to race conditions)
# article = Article.objects.get(id=1)
# article.views += 1
# article.save()

# With F expression (atomic update in SQL)
Article.objects.filter(id=1).update(views=F('views') + 1)"},{"order":12,"type":"heading","text":"3. Complex Lookups with Q Expressions"},{"order":13,"type":"paragraph","text":"When you need to execute complex database queries involving OR conditions, standard filter kwargs joined by commas will not suffice because Django treats them as AND operations. Q objects encapsulate keyword arguments to allow complex logical queries."},{"order":14,"type":"subheading","text":"Combining Q Objects with OR and NOT Operators"},{"order":15,"type":"code","text":"from django.db.models import Q

# Find articles that are either published OR have more than 1000 views, but are NOT authored by 'Admin'
complex_queryset = Article.objects.filter(
    (Q(status='published') | Q(views__gt=1000)) & ~Q(author__name='Admin')
)"},{"order":16,"type":"heading","text":"Best Practices Summary"},{"order":17,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Always return Custom QuerySets from custom managers to preserve method chaining."},{"order":18,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Use F expressions when updating records based on their existing database values to avoid concurrency bugs."},{"order":19,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Use Q objects for encapsulation of complex conditional logic, making your repository/manager layers clean and testable."}]
